emy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/2876b8d3c2d6920da03a828ab411bb489bda9743cc0a210486c725348e14f13c.png)" 13194139535671 true] [17592186147166 1145 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = \\sin(4x), \\qquad y_2 = \\cos(4x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1\\sin(4x)+u_2\\cos(4x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p'$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1(\\sin(4x))'+u_2 (\\cos(4x))'\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ by differentiating $y_p$ and equating this expression to our constraint on $y_p'$.\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= (u_{1}\\sin (4x) + u_{2}\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= (u_{1}\\sin (4x))^{′} + (u_{2}\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= u_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + u_{1}(\\sin (4x))^{′} + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}(\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= u_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for $y_p'$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= u_{1}(\\sin (4x))^{′} + u_{2}(\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) \\\\\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\nu_1' \\sin(4x) + u_2' \\cos(4x) = 0\n$$" 13194139535671 false] [17592186147166 1145 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = \\sin(4x), \\qquad y_2 = \\cos(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1\\sin(4x)+u_2\\cos(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1(\\sin(4x))'+u_2 (\\cos(4x))'.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ by differentiating $y_p$ and equating this expression to our constraint on $y_p'.$\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))}^{′}+{({u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for $y_p',$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & ={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)